I play slow and kind of re-discover any lost knowledge; but there's
not much essential to remember in my games. The reason is that I
usually stop games at certain milestones which I try to finish before
saving the game; milestones are, e.g., before/after facing the quest
nemesis, starting/finishing the castle, before/after doing Orcus town,
Vlad, Moloch's sanctum, before the Planes, etc. Also pre-quest there's
not much to think of in a restarted game. In some cases, if I have to
leave a game, I make a small note about the next one or two tasks that
I want to do next, but that's a rare case. In case that there's some
danger to be aware of on the next/previous level I do some engraving
at the stairs as a warning.

It's not uncommon for me to have a game delayed for two weeks (or even
two months). Engraving notes during the game, naming objects, playing
slow, and inspecting inventory and discoveries are key components of a
successful game resumption.

The bigger problem is to restart a game if I am in no good mood; then
death is more likely. Abstaining from a game is better than playing in
such a case.

